<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gabriel Bethlen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a>–<a href="/years/1629">1629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Gábor Bethlen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gabriel Bethlen was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a> from 1613 to 1629 and, briefly, elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. A Calvinist ruling under Ottoman suzerainty, he joined the Protestant side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. At home he strengthened princely finances and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a> a centre of Protestant learning with a new college.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gabriel-bethlen.jpg" alt="Gabriel Bethlen" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gabor_Bethlen-Hungary_National_Musem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gabriel was the elder of the two sons of Farkas Bethlen de Iktár, a Hungarian nobleman who had lost his ancestral estate to the Ottoman occupation, and Druzsiána Lázár de Szárhegy, who came from a Székely noble family. He was born at Marosillye, now Ilia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Both parents died in 1591, and the two orphaned brothers were placed under the guardianship of their maternal uncle, András Lázár de Szárhegy, a soldier who paid little attention to their formal education.</p><p>Little is documented of his youth, since only two documents written between 1593 and 1602 mention him. Modern historians assume that Stephen Bocskai, whom Gabriel called his kin, advanced his career at the court of Sigismund Báthory. He was wounded at the Battle of Sellenberk in 1599, fought against the commander Giorgio Basta's rule, and after the defeat at Tövis in 1602 fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, where the refugees came to regard him as their leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bethlen persuaded Stephen Bocskai to rise against the commissioners of Emperor Rudolph, and Bocskai was elected prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> on 21 February 1605. Bethlen then received the domain of Vajdahunyad and became perpetual ispán of Hunyad County. He was a Calvinist who helped the Jesuit György Káldy translate and print the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, composed hymns and from 1625 employed Johannes Thesselius as kapellmeister. He later fell out with Prince Gabriel Báthory and fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. After Báthory was murdered in 1613, the Ottomans installed Bethlen, and the Diet at Kolozsvár endorsed him on 13 October 1613.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His rule was one of enlightened absolutism. He developed mines and industry, nationalised many branches of foreign trade and built a new palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a>. He gave hereditary nobility to Protestant priests, founded the Bethlen Gabor College and sent Transylvanian students to Protestant universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He also ensured the right of serfs' children to be educated.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a> in August 1619, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Kassa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> and was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at Besztercebánya on 20 August 1620. By the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulov">Nikolsburg</a> of 31 December 1621 he renounced the title in return for religious freedoms for Hungarian Protestants, seven counties and the title of Imperial Prince. Further campaigns in 1623-1624 and 1626 ended with the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and the Peace of Pressburg. He died on 15 November 1629, and his second wife, Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, became princess regnant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gabriel Bethlen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stephen Báthory became king of Poland in 1576, and <b>Gabriel Bethlen</b> made Gyulafehérvár a centre of Protestant learning.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ilia, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Alba Iulia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.93,22.65&amp;year=1611" aria-label="Ilia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5086 546 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gabriel Bethlen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5536 816h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5536 816h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.93,22.65&amp;year=1611">What was here in 1611? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
